---
// Common Concerns. Built from the user-pasted code blocks: hero (tls-hero-wrap,
// hero photo from live site), the 18-question decision-matrix body (desktop
// two-column question list + answer panels, mobile carousel), and a
// page-specific footer service-area paragraph.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const title = 'Common Concerns — TLS Foundations';
const summary =
  "\"Foundation repair Albuquerque,\" \"foundation repair cost,\" \"free foundation inspection\" -- these are among the most expensive search terms in New Mexico, because contractors pay for them knowing homeowners search when they're worried and don't know where to turn. This page answers the eighteen questions behind those searches directly -- cost, warning signs, settlement vs. heave, insurance coverage, warranties, how to choose a company -- instead of routing that worry into a sales call. Click any question to see the honest answer, no contact form required first.";
const description =
  "Eighteen questions New Mexico homeowners actually search for. Answered honestly -- cost, signs, settlement vs. heave, insurance, warranties, and more.";

const serviceAreaText = `This page exists because the most common foundation repair search terms in New Mexico — foundation repair Albuquerque, foundation repair cost, signs of foundation problems, free foundation inspection — lead homeowners to sales calls, not answers. TLS Foundations is a New Mexico foundation repair contractor. We answer these questions honestly because homeowners who understand their situation make better decisions, ask better questions, and are better to work with. The eighteen questions on this page cover foundation repair cost in New Mexico, how to recognize foundation problems, the difference between settlement and heave, when foundation repair is not the right answer, how to choose a foundation contractor, what expansive soil does to a house, whether homeowners insurance covers foundation repair, and what happens when the wrong repair method is used. If your question isn't here, call or text — you reach the owner.`;

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    ['How much does foundation repair cost in New Mexico?', 'Cost depends on soil, depth, and method. A price given before investigation is a guess. Helical piers in residential work typically range from $1,500–$5,000 per pier, but the number depends on soil investigation.'],
    ['What are the signs of foundation problems?', 'Diagonal cracks at door/window corners, sticking doors or windows, sloping floors, gaps at trim, stair-step cracks in brick, water pooling near foundation.'],
    ['Do I need a structural engineer or a foundation company?', "A free inspection is a sales call. An engineer's investigation is independent of repair sales. If you're making a larger financial decision, an independent assessment matters."],
    ["What's the difference between settlement and heave?", 'Settlement = sinking. Heave = lifting. Symptoms look identical. Repairs are opposite. Installing settlement piers on a heaving foundation makes it worse.'],
    ['Is a free foundation inspection really free?', "The inspection is free because the business model recoups the cost on the repair. It's a sales presentation, not an independent assessment. Ask who inspects your home."],
    ['Does homeowners insurance cover foundation repair?', "Almost never for soil movement — settlement, heave, expansive clay are excluded. Possible for sudden plumbing breaks, but rare."],
    ['Will foundation problems get worse if I wait?', 'It depends. Seasonal movement can stabilize. Some movement is cosmetic. If movement is active and progressive, act. If stable, monitoring is legitimate.'],
    ['How do I choose a foundation repair company in New Mexico?', 'Ask: Who inspects? Licensed professional or commissioned salesperson? Can you recommend monitoring? Have you worked in my specific soil type and region?'],
    ['What causes foundation problems in New Mexico?', 'Expansive clay, collapsible soil, caliche, poor drainage, monsoon/drought cycles. Sixteen geologic regions — each behaves differently.'],
    ['How long does foundation repair take?', 'Depends on the scope of work; two to three piers a day should be completed. Drainage correction varies. We tell you before we start, not after.'],
    ['Can I sell my house with foundation problems in New Mexico?', 'Yes, but disclosure laws apply. Repair or sell as-is depends on severity. We give you the honest picture of what the problem is.'],
    ['Does foundation repair come with a warranty?', 'Yes — but what does it actually cover? Who enforces it if the company is acquired? Read the fine print. A transferable warranty is standard.'],
    ['What is expansive soil and how does it affect my house?', 'Clay soil that swells when wet, shrinks when dry. Pushes foundations up (heave). Without testing, no one can tell you if your soil is expansive.'],
    ['When is "monitor and wait" the right answer?', "When movement is cosmetic, stable, or seasonal. Not every crack needs repair. Monitoring is a legitimate recommendation."],
    ['What happens if the wrong repair method is used?', 'Installing settlement piers on a heaving foundation makes it worse. Misdiagnosis is real. Investigation before repair is the only protection.'],
    ['What are the different foundation repair methods?', 'Helical piers, push piers, drainage correction, poly foam, carbon fiber, etc. The method follows the investigation.'],
    ['Will foundation repair fix my sticking doors or cracked walls?', 'Structural repair stabilizes the foundation. Cosmetic finishes may not return to perfect alignment. We explain what to expect before you commit.'],
    ['Will watering my foundation help?', 'In some cases, consistent moisture can reduce clay shrinkage. In others, it triggers heave. Do not start a watering regimen without understanding your soil type.'],
  ].map(([q, a]) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: a } })),
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  footer="full"
  ogImage="/images/common-concerns-hero.webp"
  summary={summary}
  pageName="Common Concerns"
  nextPage={{ path: '/foundation-repair-methods', name: 'Foundation Repair Methods' }}
  footerProps={{
    serviceAreaLabel: "Six methods (not to mention a couple others we have up our sleeves). The right one depends on your soil.",
    serviceAreaText,
  }}
  schema={[faqSchema]}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <img class="hero-photo" src="/images/common-concerns-hero.webp" alt="New Mexico adobe home foundation" fetchpriority="high" />
    <div class="hero-overlay"></div>
    <div class="tls-hero-wrap">
      <div class="tls-hero-box">
        <span class="tls-hero-headline">Google says these are important.</span>
        <span class="tls-hero-sub">(So we answered them.)</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="decision-matrix">
    <div class="matrix-header">
      <h1 class="matrix-title">Common Concerns</h1>
      <p class="matrix-description">Eighteen questions New Mexico homeowners actually search for. Answered honestly.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mission-note">
      <p>The most common search terms for foundation repair in New Mexico are: "Foundation repair Albuquerque." "Foundation repair Rio Rancho." "Foundation repair Santa Fe." "Foundation repair Las Cruces." Those are among the most expensive search terms in New Mexico — contractors pay for them because homeowners search them when they're worried and don't know where to turn. Every click on that keyword costs someone money. This page doesn't. It exists because the answer to that search shouldn't be a sales call — it should be honest information about what's happening under your house, what it means, and what your options actually are. That's what's here.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mobile-carousel">
      <div class="mobile-question-nav">
        <button class="mobile-prev" id="prevBtn">← Previous</button>
        <span class="mobile-counter" id="mobileCounter">1 of 18</span>
        <button class="mobile-next" id="nextBtn">Next →</button>
      </div>
      <div class="mobile-question-list" id="mobileQuestionList"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="matrix-two-col">
      <div class="question-list-col desktop-only">
        <div class="question-list" id="questionList">
          <button class="question-btn active" data-q="cost">How much does foundation repair cost in New Mexico?</button>
          <button class="question-btn" data-q="signs">What are the signs of foundation problems?</button>
          <button class="question-btn" data-q="engineer">Do I need a structural engineer or a foundation company?</button>
          <button class="question-btn" data-q="heave">What's the difference between settlement and heave?</button>
          <button class="question-btn" data-q="free-inspection">Is a free foundation inspection really free?</button>
          <button class="question-btn" data-q="insurance">Does homeowners insurance cover foundation repair?</button>
          <button class="question-btn" data-q="wait">Will foundation problems get worse if I wait?</button>
          <button class="question-btn" data-q="choose">How do I choose a foundation repair company in New Mexico?</button>
          <button class="question-btn" data-q="causes">What causes foundation problems in New Mexico?</button>
          <button class="question-btn" data-q="timeline">How long does foundation repair take?</button>
          <button class="question-btn" data-q="sell">Can I sell my house with foundation problems in New Mexico?</button>
          <button class="question-btn" data-q="warranty">Does foundation repair come with a warranty?</button>
          <button class="question-btn" data-q="expansive">What is expansive soil and how does it affect my house?</button>
          <button class="question-btn" data-q="monitor">When is "monitor and wait" the right answer?</button>
          <button class="question-btn" data-q="wrong-repair">What happens if the wrong repair method is used?</button>
          <button class="question-btn" data-q="methods">What are the different foundation repair methods?</button>
          <button class="question-btn" data-q="doors-after">Will foundation repair fix my sticking doors or cracked walls?</button>
          <button class="question-btn" data-q="watering">Will watering my foundation help?</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="answer-card-col">
        <div id="answerPanel_cost" class="answer-panel">
          <div class="answer-card">
            <h2 class="card-question">How much does foundation repair cost in New Mexico?</h2>
            <div class="card-answer">
              <p>Cost depends on soil, depth, and method. A price given before investigation is a guess. In New Mexico, pier depth varies dramatically by region — a 20‑foot pier in the South Valley and a 20‑foot pier in the East Mountains are not the same job.</p>
              <div class="nm-note">NM context: Helical piers in residential work typically range from $1,500–$5,000 per pier, but the number depends on soil investigation, primarily depth of problematic soils.</div>
              <div class="industry-note">No reputable contractor can give you a price over the phone without knowing your soil. Anyone who does is selling a system, not solving your problem.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="card-links">
              <div class="primary-links">
                <a href="/foundation-repair-methods" class="card-link">Repair Methods →</a>
                <a href="/contact-us" class="card-link">Get an evaluation →</a>
              </div>
              <div class="related-terms"><span>Related terms</span><a href="/glossary#helical-pier">Helical pier</a><a href="/glossary#push-pier">Push pier</a><a href="/glossary#soil-investigation">Soil investigation</a><a href="/glossary#bearing-capacity">Bearing capacity</a></div>
              <div class="related-faq"><span>Related questions</span><a href="/common-concerns#timeline">How long does repair take?</a><a href="/common-concerns#engineer">Do I need an engineer?</a></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="answerPanel_signs" class="answer-panel" style="display:none">
          <div class="answer-card">
            <h2 class="card-question">What are the signs of foundation problems?</h2>
            <div class="card-answer">
              <p>Diagonal cracks at door/window corners, sticking doors or windows, sloping floors, gaps at trim, stair‑step cracks in brick, water pooling near foundation.</p>
              <div class="nm-note">NM context: The same symptom can mean heave or settlement — they require opposite repairs. Signs point toward investigation, not toward a specific repair.</div>
              <div class="industry-note">The industry knows symptoms are ambiguous, but they rarely explain this. A crack from heave and a crack from settlement look nearly identical. The wrong repair makes it worse.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="card-links">
              <div class="primary-links">
                <a href="/expansive-soil" class="card-link">Expansive Soils in NM →</a>
                <a href="/collapsible-soil" class="card-link">Collapsible Soils in NM →</a>
              </div>
              <div class="related-terms"><span>Related terms</span><a href="/glossary#heave">Heave</a><a href="/glossary#settlement">Settlement</a><a href="/glossary#expansive-soil">Expansive soil</a><a href="/glossary#collapsible-soil">Collapsible soil</a></div>
              <div class="related-faq"><span>Related questions</span><a href="/common-concerns#heave">What's the difference between settlement and heave?</a><a href="/common-concerns#wait">Is this serious?</a></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="answerPanel_engineer" class="answer-panel" style="display:none">
          <div class="answer-card">
            <h2 class="card-question">Do I need a structural engineer or a foundation company?</h2>
            <div class="card-answer">
              <p>A free inspection is a sales call. An engineer's investigation is independent of repair sales. If you're making a larger financial decision, an independent assessment matters.</p>
              <div class="nm-note">NM context: Our affiliate Sandia GEO does geotechnical investigation separate from repair sales — investigation revenue is intentionally separate.</div>
              <div class="industry-note">The person inspecting your home for a free inspection is compensated to sell repairs. That's the business model — not a criticism, just a fact worth understanding before you make a large financial decision.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="card-links">
              <div class="primary-links">
                <a href="/our-approach" class="card-link">Why NM's Different →</a>
                <a href="/contact-us" class="card-link">Contact Sandia GEO →</a>
              </div>
              <div class="related-terms"><span>Related terms</span><a href="/glossary#geotechnical-investigation">Geotechnical investigation</a><a href="/glossary#structural-engineer">Structural engineer</a><a href="/glossary#free-inspection">Free inspection</a><a href="/glossary#conflict-of-interest">Conflict of interest</a></div>
              <div class="related-faq"><span>Related questions</span><a href="/common-concerns#free-inspection">Is a free inspection really free?</a><a href="/common-concerns#choose">How do I choose a company?</a></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="answerPanel_heave" class="answer-panel" style="display:none">
          <div class="answer-card">
            <h2 class="card-question">What's the difference between settlement and heave?</h2>
            <div class="card-answer">
              <p>Settlement = sinking. Heave = lifting. Symptoms look identical. Repairs are opposite. Installing settlement piers on a heaving foundation makes it worse.</p>
              <div class="nm-note">NM context: Expansive clay soils cause heave. Collapsible soils cause settlement. Both are common in New Mexico.</div>
              <div class="industry-note">Misdiagnosis in this industry is more common than anyone admits. Heave treated as settlement — piers installed on a lifting foundation — makes the problem worse and the repair irreversible. Investigation before repair is the only protection.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="card-links">
              <div class="primary-links">
                <a href="/expansive-soil" class="card-link">Expansive Soils →</a>
                <a href="/collapsible-soil" class="card-link">Collapsible Soils →</a>
              </div>
              <div class="related-terms"><span>Related terms</span><a href="/glossary#expansive-clay">Expansive clay</a><a href="/glossary#swell-pressure">Swell pressure</a><a href="/glossary#active-zone">Active zone</a><a href="/glossary#moisture-trigger">Moisture trigger</a></div>
              <div class="related-faq"><span>Related questions</span><a href="/common-concerns#causes">What causes foundation problems?</a><a href="/common-concerns#watering">Will watering my foundation help?</a></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="answerPanel_free-inspection" class="answer-panel" style="display:none">
          <div class="answer-card">
            <h2 class="card-question">Is a free foundation inspection really free?</h2>
            <div class="card-answer">
              <p>The inspection is free because the business model recoups the cost on the repair. It's a sales presentation, not an independent assessment. Ask who inspects your home.</p>
              <div class="nm-note">NM context: A paid geotechnical investigation produces an independent report. The distinction matters when you're making a financial decision.</div>
              <div class="industry-note">The 'free inspection' is the industry's lead‑gen machine. The person showing up works for the company proposing the repair. Every visit that doesn't end in a sale is a loss. That structure shapes every recommendation.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="card-links">
              <div class="primary-links">
                <a href="/our-approach" class="card-link">Why NM's Different →</a>
              </div>
              <div class="related-terms"><span>Related terms</span><a href="/glossary#free-estimate">Free estimate</a><a href="/glossary#sales-inspection">Sales inspection</a><a href="/glossary#geotechnical-investigation">Geotechnical investigation</a></div>
              <div class="related-faq"><span>Related questions</span><a href="/common-concerns#engineer">Do I need an engineer?</a><a href="/common-concerns#choose">How do I choose a company?</a></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="answerPanel_insurance" class="answer-panel" style="display:none">
          <div class="answer-card">
            <h2 class="card-question">Does homeowners insurance cover foundation repair?</h2>
            <div class="card-answer">
              <p>Almost never for soil movement — settlement, heave, expansive clay are excluded. Possible for sudden plumbing breaks, but rare. Don't make decisions expecting coverage.</p>
              <div class="nm-note">NM context: Standard policies exclude earth movement. Read your policy before you call anyone.</div>
              <div class="industry-note">Insurance coverage for foundation problems is rarely what homeowners hope it will be. Standard policies exclude earth movement. Understand your policy before anyone else shapes the conversation.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="card-links">
              <div class="primary-links">
              </div>
              <div class="related-terms"><span>Related terms</span><a href="/glossary#earth-movement-exclusion">Earth movement exclusion</a><a href="/glossary#plumbing-leak-coverage">Plumbing leak coverage</a></div>
              <div class="related-faq"><span>Related questions</span><a href="/common-concerns#sell">Can I sell my house with foundation problems?</a></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="answerPanel_wait" class="answer-panel" style="display:none">
          <div class="answer-card">
            <h2 class="card-question">Will foundation problems get worse if I wait?</h2>
            <div class="card-answer">
              <p>It depends. Seasonal movement can stabilize. Some movement is cosmetic. If movement is active and progressive, act. If stable, monitoring is legitimate.</p>
              <div class="nm-note">NM context: Monsoon/drought cycles cause normal seasonal movement. Not every crack needs repair.</div>
              <div class="industry-note">Urgency is the industry's default setting. Sometimes it's warranted. Sometimes movement is seasonal and stabilizes on its own. The honest answer depends on what's causing the movement — not on a sales timeline.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="card-links">
              <div class="primary-links">
                <a href="/foundation-repair-methods" class="card-link">Repair Methods →</a>
              </div>
              <div class="related-terms"><span>Related terms</span><a href="/glossary#active-movement">Active movement</a><a href="/glossary#seasonal-movement">Seasonal movement</a><a href="/glossary#monitoring">Monitoring</a></div>
              <div class="related-faq"><span>Related questions</span><a href="/common-concerns#monitor">When is monitoring the right answer?</a><a href="/common-concerns#wait">Is this serious?</a></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="answerPanel_choose" class="answer-panel" style="display:none">
          <div class="answer-card">
            <h2 class="card-question">How do I choose a foundation repair company in New Mexico?</h2>
            <div class="card-answer">
              <p>Ask: Who inspects? Licensed professional or commissioned salesperson? Can you recommend monitoring? Have you worked in my specific soil type and region?</p>
              <div class="nm-note">NM context: The answers tell you more than any review count. Does the company know NM soils?</div>
              <div class="industry-note">The best question you can ask: 'Can you recommend an independent structural engineer before you give me a proposal?' A company that says yes is different. A company that hesitates is telling you something.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="card-links">
              <div class="primary-links">
                <a href="/our-approach" class="card-link">Why NM's Different →</a>
              </div>
              <div class="related-terms"><span>Related terms</span><a href="/glossary#free-inspection">Free inspection</a><a href="/glossary#sales-metric">Sales metric</a><a href="/glossary#scope-of-work">Scope of work</a></div>
              <div class="related-faq"><span>Related questions</span><a href="/common-concerns#engineer">Do I need an engineer?</a><a href="/common-concerns#free-inspection">Is a free inspection really free?</a></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="answerPanel_causes" class="answer-panel" style="display:none">
          <div class="answer-card">
            <h2 class="card-question">What causes foundation problems in New Mexico?</h2>
            <div class="card-answer">
              <p>Expansive clay, collapsible soil, caliche, poor drainage, monsoon/drought cycles. Sixteen geologic regions — each behaves differently.</p>
              <div class="nm-note">NM context: Mancos Shale in the Four Corners is highly expansive. Collapsible soils are common in the Rio Grande valley.</div>
              <div class="industry-note">National franchises train inspectors on generic soil conditions. New Mexico has sixteen distinct geologic regions. A training manual can't prepare someone for what's under your house.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="card-links">
              <div class="primary-links">
                <a href="/new-mexico-soil-conditions" class="card-link">NM Soils Hub →</a>
                <a href="/expansive-soil" class="card-link">Expansive Soils →</a>
                <a href="/collapsible-soil" class="card-link">Collapsible Soils →</a>
              </div>
              <div class="related-terms"><span>Related terms</span><a href="/glossary#mancos-shale">Mancos Shale</a><a href="/glossary#caliche">Caliche</a><a href="/glossary#alluvial-soils">Alluvial soils</a><a href="/glossary#karst">Karst</a></div>
              <div class="related-faq"><span>Related questions</span><a href="/common-concerns#heave">What's the difference between settlement and heave?</a><a href="/common-concerns#expansive">What is expansive soil?</a></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="answerPanel_timeline" class="answer-panel" style="display:none">
          <div class="answer-card">
            <h2 class="card-question">How long does foundation repair take?</h2>
            <div class="card-answer">
              <p>Helical piers: Depends on the scope of work two to three piers a day should be completed. Drainage correction varies. Depends on scope. We tell you before we start, not after.</p>
              <div class="nm-note">NM context: Soil complexity can affect timeline — reaching bearing in some formations takes longer than national averages.</div>
              <div class="industry-note">A company that gives you a firm timeline before investigating your soil is guessing. Depth requirements vary. Access matters. We tell you after we know what we're working with.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="card-links">
              <div class="primary-links">
              </div>
              <div class="related-terms"><span>Related terms</span><a href="/glossary#helical-pier">Helical pier</a><a href="/glossary#drainage-correction">Drainage correction</a></div>
              <div class="related-faq"><span>Related questions</span><a href="/common-concerns#cost">How much does it cost?</a><a href="/common-concerns#methods">What methods are there?</a></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="answerPanel_sell" class="answer-panel" style="display:none">
          <div class="answer-card">
            <h2 class="card-question">Can I sell my house with foundation problems in New Mexico?</h2>
            <div class="card-answer">
              <p>Yes, but disclosure laws apply. Repair or sell as‑is depends on severity. We give you the honest picture of what the problem is.</p>
              <div class="nm-note">NM context: Disclosure requirements vary by county. Know what you're required to disclose.</div>
              <div class="industry-note">You can sell a home with foundation issues. Whether repair improves your net position depends on severity, buyer pool, and market conditions. Get the honest picture of what the problem is before anyone tells you what you have to do.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="card-links">
              <div class="primary-links">
              </div>
              <div class="related-terms"><span>Related terms</span><a href="/glossary#disclosure">Disclosure</a><a href="/glossary#home-inspection">Home inspection</a><a href="/glossary#value-impact">Value impact</a></div>
              <div class="related-faq"><span>Related questions</span><a href="/common-concerns#insurance">Does insurance cover foundation repair?</a><a href="/common-concerns#wait">Is this serious?</a></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="answerPanel_warranty" class="answer-panel" style="display:none">
          <div class="answer-card">
            <h2 class="card-question">Does foundation repair come with a warranty?</h2>
            <div class="card-answer">
              <p>Yes — but what does it actually cover? Who enforces it if the company is acquired? Read the fine print. A transferable warranty is standard.</p>
              <div class="nm-note">NM context: National warranties look strong until the local operation changes hands. We explain our warranty honestly, including limitations.</div>
              <div class="industry-note">A warranty is only as good as the company backing it. When a national franchise gets acquired, the local office changes. Your warranty? Read the transfer terms carefully.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="card-links">
              <div class="primary-links">
              </div>
              <div class="related-terms"><span>Related terms</span><a href="/glossary#transferable-warranty">Transferable warranty</a><a href="/glossary#lifetime-warranty">Lifetime warranty</a><a href="/glossary#workmanship-warranty">Workmanship warranty</a></div>
              <div class="related-faq"><span>Related questions</span><a href="/common-concerns#choose">How do I choose a company?</a><a href="/common-concerns#wrong-repair">What happens if the wrong repair is used?</a></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="answerPanel_expansive" class="answer-panel" style="display:none">
          <div class="answer-card">
            <h2 class="card-question">What is expansive soil and how does it affect my house?</h2>
            <div class="card-answer">
              <p>Clay soil that swells when wet, shrinks when dry. Pushes foundations up (heave). Without testing, no one can tell you if your soil is expansive.</p>
              <div class="nm-note">NM context: Common in the Albuquerque Basin, Four Corners, and Rio Grande Valley.</div>
              <div class="industry-note">A contractor who looks at your house and tells you 'you have expansive soil' without testing is guessing. Expansive clay requires lab testing to confirm. Guessing is not engineering.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="card-links">
              <div class="primary-links">
                <a href="/expansive-soil" class="card-link">Expansive Soils in NM →</a>
                <a href="/new-mexico-soil-conditions" class="card-link">NM Soils →</a>
              </div>
